Some people say that maybe he’s too old to be coaching a college ‘baseball team. But Andy Cohen doesn’t think he’s too old. Maybe
that’s why he comes back year after year as UT El Paso’s head baseball mentor.
“T love the sport very much,” says Cohen. “It gives me a thrill to see one of my ball players become a high school coach just as it does
when one of’ them signs with the pros.” ;
Cohen has been in baseball for a long time. His name is synonymous with the sport in El Paso. 
But it has been quite a while since he has produced a winning team at UT El Paso. In his 12 years as the Miner head coach his overall
record stands on the negative side.
12 years of losing? Grounds for impeachment perhaps? = Most certainly for coaches of other sports. But Cohen is the baseball coach and at UT El Paso that means little or nothing.
Baseball at one time was the national pastime but in Minerland it has always run, a distant fourth to football, basketball and track. As UT EI Paso is considered the step child of the Texas state college system, baseball is then the step child of a step child. How low on the totem pole can one get?

Well, unfostunately baseball ranks at the very bottom. And it will probably remain there unless something drastic is done. A house-cleaning is needed and a new
program should be installed.
Andy Cohen has been good for baseball and baseball has been extremely good to Cohen. But a new and winning attitude needs to be instituted to help baseball rise
from its rock botiom status. The sport cannot afford to dig itself deeper into the hole of oblivion.

